  Next fork, and it's forced by the repo having no bundler: [1mhow [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gets hold of the settings module.[0m

  - [1mSecond script tag + global[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m loads before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and exposes [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m. Buys you:
    matches the repo's current zero-tooling style exactly, still opens straight from [38;5;153mfile://[39m, works in any browser.
    Costs: one more global on [38;5;153mwindow[39m, and load order in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes load-bearing. Trivial to reverse.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m with [38;5;153mimport { settings } from './settings.js'[39m. Buys you: real
    imports, no globals, explicit dependencies. Costs: ES modules are blocked by CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops working — you'd need a local server to run it at all. Module scripts also defer,
    which is fine here but changes when your [38;5;153maddEventListener[39m runs. Reversible, but it changes how anyone runs the
    page.
  - [1mDual export (global + CommonJS)[0m — same file usable by the browser and by [38;5;153msrc/[39m's [38;5;153mrequire[39m. Buys you: one settings
    source for both halves of the repo. Costs: a conditional-export wrapper, for a Node entry point that currently has
    nothing to do with the API.

  [1mMy recommendation: second script tag + global.[0m It's the only option that doesn't change how you run the app, and the
  repo has no dependencies or build step to suggest you want the ES-module path yet. The dual export is speculative —
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m just prints a greeting.
